course: dictionary
lesson: 11536
title: Formal words in the news
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words sound grown-up because newspapers, police and official reports use them. Altercation is one of these. When you see it, look at the words around it to find out what happened. «There was an altercation outside the school gate after the bell.» Outside a school gate after the bell, people were arguing noisily or having a small fight.
explain: Writers choose this word on purpose. It sounds polite, so it tells you that people argued loudly or pushed each other, but it does not say how bad it was. «The police said two men were involved in an altercation in the high street.» The police are being careful with their words, so they give the facts without the details.
explain: Look for the extra word that tells you what kind of altercation it was. «The argument was only words, a verbal altercation, and nobody was hurt.» Here verbal means only words. «A physical altercation» would mean people pushed or hit each other. Always check the clues before you decide how serious it was.
fact: Altercation means a noisy argument or a small fight between people.
fact: It is a formal word, so you will mostly meet it in news reports, police statements and school reports.
fact: Reporters and police often use altercation for a scuffle when they do not want to give details.
fact: A verbal altercation is only words. A physical altercation involves pushing or hitting.
fact: Phrases to know: «be involved in an altercation» and «an altercation broke out», which means it started suddenly.
exercise: «The police said two men were involved in an altercation in the high street.» What does altercation mean? | *A noisy argument or small fight | A kind gift from a friend | A school trip to the museum | A quiet walk together
exercise: «An altercation broke out when the two teams argued about the goal.» Find and copy one word that shows it started suddenly. | goal | argued | teams | *broke
exercise: A news report says «a verbal altercation». What does verbal mean here? | Written down on paper | Sung loudly in a song | *Only words, with no pushing or hitting | Using a ball and a bat
test: «There was a physical altercation in the playground.» What does physical mean in this sentence? | Talking loudly | *Pushing or hitting | Lost and found | Happy and friendly
test: Why might a news report say altercation instead of fight? | It means a football match | *It is a polite, formal word that can hide how bad it really was | It means a happy party | It is a word only used by children
exam: A police officer writes, «Two people were involved in an altercation.» What does the officer most likely mean? | A long walk in the park | A prize they won together | A friendly chat about the weather | *A noisy argument or small fight, described politely
